<Layout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical}>
<Fragment slot="head-scripts">
  {schemaJsons.map((schema) => (<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />))}
</Fragment>

<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="washer-repair/ge-error-codes"
  eyebrow="GE Washer Messages & Codes · Diagnostic-First · LA Home Repair · Same Day"
  title="GE Washer Error Codes, Decoded (Mostly, It's Plain English)"
  subtitle={`GE usually tells you in words, not codes. We fix GE washers same-day across Los Angeles, Orange, Ventura, Santa Barbara, San Bernardino, Riverside. $89 residential diagnostic. ${phone}`}
  heroImageTitle="GE Washer Error Code Repair"
/>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Answer first</p>
    <h2>The honest version: GE mostly doesn't use cryptic codes, and the E-code chart you found is probably Electrolux.</h2>

    <p><strong>Unlike Samsung and LG, most GE washers speak in plain language — "Confirm Water Supply," "Check and Clean Drain Pump Filter," an unbalanced-load light — instead of codes like 5C or OE. So there's no big GE code chart to memorize, and that's by design. Two exceptions: the newer GE <span class="code">UltraFast</span> combo uses model-specific numeric codes, and older GE <span class="code">Profile</span> front-loaders used an E-series (technician-level). And the important myth-buster: <span class="code">E10 / E20 / E40 / E60 / E90</span> are Electrolux and Frigidaire codes, not GE — a lot of code sites mislabel them. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair. BHGS #A49573, EPA 608 Universal certified (#1346255700410), BBB Accredited.</strong></p>

    <p>Here's why this page is shorter than our Samsung or LG code pages: there's simply less to decode. When a GE washer stops, it usually prints the problem category in words, which puts you most of the way to the fix already. Our job is to read that message plus test on-site, because "Confirm Water Supply" could be an open valve you forgot or a failed inlet valve — the words tell you the category, not the exact part.</p>

    <p>Below: what GE's plain-language messages mean, the two code exceptions (UltraFast, Profile), and the Electrolux mix-up in full. Parent reference: <a href="/services/washer-repair/error-codes/">all washer error codes</a>. Brand pillar: <a href="/brands/ge-washer-repair/">GE washer repair</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Plain-language messages</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">What GE's words mean, and what they cost to fix.</h2>
    <p class="lead">GE tells you the category. These map to the same faults the other brands hide behind codes.</p>
    <table class="price-table">
      <thead><tr><th>GE says</th><th>Category</th><th>Where to start</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Confirm Water Supply / won't fill</td><td>Fill / water supply</td><td>Valves open, hoses, inlet screens, then inlet valve ($260-380)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Check and Clean Drain Pump Filter / won't drain</td><td>Drain</td><td>Filter (if equipped), hose, drain pump ($380-540)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Unbalanced load / spin light</td><td>Unbalance</td><td>Redistribute load; suspension if persistent ($440-640)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Door / lid won't lock</td><td>Lock</td><td>Nothing pinched in the seal; then interlock ($260-440)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Too many suds</td><td>Suds</td><td>Cut detergent, use HE soap (customer-side)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Lid open</td><td>Lid switch</td><td>Close firmly; lid switch if it persists</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Related symptom pages: <a href="/services/washer-repair/not-draining/">washer not draining</a> · <a href="/services/washer-repair/wont-spin/">washer won't spin</a> · <a href="/services/washer-repair/leaking/">washer leaking</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">The Electrolux mix-up</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">E10 / E20 / E40 / E60 are NOT GE codes.</h2>
    <p>This one causes real confusion, so it's worth being blunt. If you found a chart listing E10 (inlet), E20 (drain), E40 (door), E60 (motor), E70 (sensor), or E90 (control) under "GE washer codes," that chart is wrong — those are <strong>Electrolux and Frigidaire</strong> codes. Aggregator sites lump brands together and mislabel them constantly.</p>
    <p>So if your washer is genuinely showing E20 or E40, check the nameplate. It's almost certainly a Frigidaire or Electrolux machine, and the correct table lives in the Frigidaire/Electrolux section of our <a href="/services/washer-repair/error-codes/">main washer error-codes page</a>. GE simply doesn't use that E10/E20/E40 scheme — its front-loaders that do use E-numbers are the older Profile series described below, and those mean different things.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">The two code exceptions</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">UltraFast combo (numeric) and older Profile (E-series).</h2>
    <p><strong>GE UltraFast combo:</strong> the newer all-in-one washer/dryer combo does use numeric fault codes. They're specific to that unit and shift by model and firmware, so we don't reproduce a chart that might not match yours — our techs read the code off your machine and cross-reference GE's current service docs for that exact model. If your UltraFast is showing a number, tell us when you call and we come prepared.</p>
    <p><strong>Older GE Profile front-loaders:</strong> these used an E-series (roughly E1 through the E20s) for motor, control, and sensor faults. It's a technician-level scheme — the diagnosis needs a meter — and it is <em>not</em> the Electrolux E10/E20/E40 set despite the surface resemblance. An aging Profile flashing an E-number is worth a service visit, and at that age we'll give you the honest repair-versus-replace read.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">DIY first steps</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before you book a visit.</h2>
    <ol class="num-list">
      <li><strong>Read the words.</strong> GE usually names the category. "Confirm Water Supply" → check valves and hoses. "Check and Clean Drain Pump Filter" → clear the filter.</li>
      <li><strong>Power cycle.</strong> Unplug for 60 seconds (or flip the breaker), restore power, run a short cycle.</li>
      <li><strong>Drain filter (if equipped).</strong> Lower-front access door on models that have one, pan underneath, clear debris.</li>
      <li><strong>Inlet hose &amp; screen.</strong> Valves open, hoses not kinked, mesh screens clear.</li>
      <li><strong>Redistribute the load.</strong> Unbalance and spin-light messages usually clear when you spread the laundry.</li>
      <li><strong>Detergent dose.</strong> For suds messages, cut the amount and use HE detergent.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If a message returns right after you've addressed the obvious cause, that's a real fault — book a visit.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Honest opinion</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Repair a young GE, think hard about an old one.</h2>
    <p>A GE washer in its first 6 to 8 years with a single failure — a drain pump, an inlet valve, a lock — is almost always worth fixing; those are bounded parts with clear costs. Once a machine hits year 10-plus and starts stacking failures, we give you the honest math at the door: the specific repair cost against a comparable new unit. We'd rather tell you to replace a tired washer than sell a part that doesn't pencil out. $89 diagnostic, waived if you go ahead with the repair.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Pricing</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Message-driven part costs.</h2>
    <p class="lead">$89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair. No emergency surcharge — same $89 anytime.</p>
    <table class="price-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Repair</th><th>Typical cost</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Diagnostic</td><td>$89, waived with repair</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Drain pump (won't-drain message)</td><td>$380 to $540</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Inlet valve (Confirm Water Supply)</td><td>$260 to $380</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Door / lid lock (won't-lock message)</td><td>$260 to $440</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Lid switch (lid-open message)</td><td>$180 to $320</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Suspension / shocks (persistent unbalance)</td><td>$440 to $640</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Motor / control (Profile E-series, UltraFast codes)</td><td>$340 to $880 (varies by fault)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Warranty</td><td>90 days parts and labor</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">GE washer message &amp; code questions.</h2>
    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (<details class="faq-item"><summary>{f.q}</summary><p>{f.a}</p></details>))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
